2. Structural Composition Frameworks & Visual Theory

Composition is the deliberate placement of visual elements within a frame to direct the viewer’s attention and evoke deliberate visual interest. While rules serve as effective foundational guides, they are non-absolute tools rather than rigid laws.

Classical Grids & Mathematical Layouts

* Rule of Thirds (Regra dos Terços): Divides the frame into a 3 \times 3 grid using two horizontal and two vertical lines, creating nine equal rectangles. Placing key focal points along these lines or at their four intersection points creates balanced images compared to default centering.
* Phi Grid: Similar to the Rule of Thirds grid, but the vertical and horizontal division lines are positioned significantly closer to the center based on golden ratio proportions. This tighter spacing reduces the tendency for subjects to feel unanchored or excessively isolated at extreme edges.
* Golden Ratio & Fibonacci Spiral (Proporção Áurea): Derived from the Fibonacci numerical sequence (1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21\dots), this logarithmic spiral mimics geometric patterns prevalent in natural structures (galaxies, hurricanes, shells). Aligning the focal point at the tightest coil of the spiral guides the eye through a natural, sweeping curve across the frame. Software tools like Adobe Lightroom allow photographers to cycle through composition overlays (using shortcut O) and rotate the spiral orientation (Shift + O).

