A. The Natural World

* Nature Photography: Capturing wild environments and wildlife. Demands advance planning and proper protective gear due to unpredictable weather and lighting.
* Landscape Photography: Captures natural terrain, forests, and deserts. Relies on tripods, small apertures (f/8–f/16), precise timing, and specific shutter speeds.
* Astrophotography: Long-exposure photography focusing on the night sky. Requires tripods, extended shutter speeds, and low noise management to map star trails, light paintings, and celestial motion.
* Storm & Lightning Photography: Captures atmospheric weather conditions. Prioritizes physical safety protocols, specialized gear, precise shutter timing, and patience.
* Pet / Animal Photography: Captures domestic pets. Demands adaptability, behavioral patience, and post-processing techniques to preserve detail in dark animal fur.
* Macro Photography: Extreme close-up imagery of insects, small birds, fruit, and fine textures. Requires dedicated macro lenses, static environments, and careful focus control due to extremely shallow depths of field.
* Flower Photography: Accessible flora photography used by beginners to master exposure balance, lighting, and low depth-of-field control on passive subjects.

B. Urban Landscapes & Structural Photography

* Architectural Photography: Highlights structural angles, gargoyles, interior lines, and design features. Employs wide-angle lenses, natural light mastery, and central vertical axis grid alignment.
* Real Estate Photography: Commercial structural capture designed to highlight residential spaces cleanly for sales and rental markets through technical lighting and gear configurations.
* Drone Photography: Utilizes unmanned aerial platforms to capture unique top-down perspectives of bridges, highways, and building footprints.
* Aerial Photography: Encompasses all high-altitude photography executed from elevated vantage points, including helicopters, rooftops, and elevated platforms.

C. People & Human Centric Photography

* Portraiture: Focuses on human subjects to tell a personal story. Requires strong interpersonal direction, light management, model rapport, and deliberate sitting/hand poses.
* Headshots (Fotografia de Perfil): A commercialized form of portraiture producing clean, professional portraits for corporate resumes, websites, and social platforms.
* Fashion Photography: High-style imagery spanning runway coverage, commercial catalogs, and creative editorials. Focuses on client directives and active model orientation.
* Sports Photography: Captures fast-paced athletic action. Spans photojournalistic live-event tracking (requiring ultra-fast shutter speeds) and commercial product placement photos for athletic brands.
* Documentary Photography: Unscripted recording of real-world events, social issues, and daily life. Prioritizes authentic, unmanipulated reality over controlled studio setups.

D. Lifestyle & Candid Photography

* Street Photography: Everyday candid life captured in public spaces (markets, parks, sidewalks). Focuses on rapid observation, quick reaction times, and unique perspectives.
* Wedding Photography: Fast-paced event coverage recording dynamic, high-stakes personal celebrations over long, grueling schedules. Demands technical mastery across diverse lighting environments.

E. Commercial & Product Photography

* Food Photography (Gastronômica): Appetizing captures of culinary dishes for advertising, restaurant menus, tourism portals, and stock photo licensing. Relies on meticulous styling and lighting control.
* Product Photography: Clean, attractive studio imagery highlighting merchandise (footwear, cosmetics, outdoor gear) for brand catalogs and e-commerce platforms.

F. Artistic & Conceptual Photography

* Still Life Photography (Natureza-Morta): Arranged artistic photos of inanimate objects (fruit bowls, curated products). Builds foundational skills directly applicable to food and product photography.
* Black & White Photography: Strips away color to emphasize fundamental composition, light contrast, tonal values, shapes, and structural form.
* Fine Art Photography (Fotografia Artística): Images driven purely by the artistic vision and emotional intent of the photographer, independent of commercial constraints.
* Double Exposure: Combines two distinct images into a single blended composite using digital editing software or in-camera tricks.
* Surrealist Photography: Creates dreamlike, provocative, or unconventional compositions using photo manipulation software and layered elements.
* Abstract Photography: Focuses on unusual viewpoints—zooming tightly onto micro-cracks or pulling back to extreme aerial scales—to reimagine familiar objects as pure shape, color, and texture.

Conclusion

Mastering photography involves combining technical knowledge with visual awareness. Photographers achieve control over their work by managing the Exposure Triangle to set accurate luminance and depth of field, selecting non-rigid compositional layouts to direct visual weight, and adjusting perspectives and aspect ratios to match their storytelling goals.
